#!omni universal

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// OMNICODE UNIVERSAL TEMPLATE
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// TEMPLATE OVERVIEW (grep: "UNIVERSAL TEMPLATE" | "WHAT STARTS MUST END" | "8-rung ladder")
//
// This is the UNIVERSAL TEMPLATE - the foundation from which all OmniCode
// templates derive. It documents the patterns common to ALL OmniCode files
// regardless of their specific type.
//
// WHAT STARTS MUST END: All OmniCode files have METADATA and CLOSING blocks.
// The MIDDLE varies by type and purpose.
//
// PROPAGATION EQUATION (grep: "PROPAGATION" | "Δsyntax" | "meta.from" | "derives from")
//
//   Δsyntax → Δuniversal → Δ[type-template] → Δ[format-template] → Δ[implementation]
//
//   CHAIN (OmniCode layer):
//     syntax.omni (B-word-omni-syntax)                    ← ROOT SPEC
//       → universal.omni (this file)                      ← UNIVERSAL TEMPLATE
//           → [type].omni                                 ← TYPE TEMPLATE
//               code.omni      (4-block executable/library)
//               interface.omni (3-block contracts)
//               data.omni      (3/4/5-block data)
//               documentation.omni (5-block docs)
//
//   CHAIN (Format layer - traditional code):
//     [type].omni                                         ← OmniCode template
//       → [format-template]                               ← Format-specific template
//           Code:  library.go, library.c, library.sh
//           Docs:  asciidoc-base.adoc, markdown-base.md
//           Data:  config.toml, schema.json
//       → [implementation]                                ← Actual files
//           Code:  loader.go, config.go, parser.c
//           Docs:  api-reference.adoc, user-guide.md
//           Data:  settings.toml, manifest.json
//
//   BIDIRECTIONAL MAPPING:
//     OmniCode:       "derives from" + "documented at"
//     Traditional:    "meta.from" (comment pragma in any format)
//
// SECTIONS (grep: "keyed as" | "grounded in" | "PREAMBLE GUIDANCE" | "TEMPLATE PATTERNS")
//
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// METADATA (UNIVERSAL)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// The METADATA block is UNIVERSAL - all OmniCode files have it.
// It establishes identity, grounding, and operational context.
//
// Structure (8-rung ladder):
//   1. Preamble - pragma, keying, classification, derivation
//   2. Core Identity - grounding, role, authorship, purpose
//   3. Interface - dependencies, integration points
//   4. Operational Context - blocking, health scoring
//
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// THIS FILE'S IDENTITY (grep: "keyed as" | "derives from" | "grounded in")

this is a template for "OmniCode Universal":
    keyed as "B-word-omni-seed-universal"
    following "Universal Pattern"

this template classifies as:
    word (1) when "pure declaration - interface, contract, type definition"
    work (0) when "implementation - executable code, library, business logic"
    fruit (-1) when "presentation - documentation, reference, guide"

this template derives from:
    original (1) when "establishing pattern beyond syntax spec"
    derived (0) from "B-word-omni-syntax" because "implements the syntax specification"
    variant (-1) when "intentionally departing from syntax spec"
    documented at "bereshit/word/omni/syntax.omni"

this template strictness is:
    tight (1) meaning "Follow exactly - structural blocks, required sections"
    guided (0) meaning "Adapt thoughtfully - content within structure"
    free (-1) meaning "Principles only - teaching comments, examples"

to use this template:
    reference for understanding OmniCode patterns
    all other templates derive from this universal foundation
    do not copy directly - use specific templates for your file type

    meaning "Universal foundation - the patterns all OmniCode files share"
    teaching "METADATA and CLOSING are universal; MIDDLE varies by type"

grounded in:
    scripture "In the beginning God created the heaven and the earth"
        from Genesis 1:1 in KJV
    principle "What starts must end - structure precedes content"
    anchor "And God saw every thing that he had made, and, behold, it was very good"
        from Genesis 1:31 in KJV

serves as:
    Rails (foundation) for "All OmniCode templates derive structure from this universal"
    role "Root template defining patterns shared by all OmniCode file types"

authored by:
    architect "Seanje Lenox-Wise"
    implementation "Nova Dawn"
    created "2025-11-01"
    version "1.0.0"
    modified "2025-12-17" with "METADATA completion, grep hints, block reordering"

exists to:
    purpose "Define universal patterns for all OmniCode files"
    features:
        "8-rung METADATA ladder (keyed as → operational context)"
        "Universal CLOSING structure (operations → synthesis)"
        "Template patterns with teaching comments"
    design "Template by example - structure IS the specification"
    philosophy "WHAT STARTS MUST END - METADATA opens, CLOSING closes"

interface of this template:
    depends on:
        requires (1): "bereshit/word/omni/syntax.omni" for "syntax specification"
        includes (0): "Genesis 1 creation pattern" for "structural foundation"
        excludes (-1): "External format dependencies - format-agnostic"
    integrated as:
        import "All OmniCode templates derive from this universal"
        pattern "Reference for structure, not copy directly"

operational context:
    blocking:
        yes (1) because "Changes here affect all downstream templates"
    health:
        system Base100 with scale from -100 to +100
        granted (1) above +50 meaning "Template is complete and aligned with syntax spec"
        deferred (0) within ±50 meaning "Template in refinement"
        denied (-1) below -50 meaning "Template has structural inconsistencies"

// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TEMPLATE PATTERNS (grep: "preamble of" | "core identity of" | "interface of" | "operational context of")
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

preamble of this [type]:
    meaning "File identity declaration - the first thing every OmniCode file establishes"
    teaching "Pragma → Identity → Classification → Derivation → Usage (for templates)"

    pragma:
        meaning "File type declaration - tells parsers what kind of OmniCode file this is"
        format "#!omni <type>" where type is <code|interface|data|documentation|template|universal>

    identity:
        meaning "What this file IS - name, key, and structure it follows"
        format "this is a <type> for <name>:"

        keyed as: <unique identifier following keying standard - B-path-to-file>
        following: <which block structure - 3-block|4-block|5-block|Universal Pattern>

    classification:
        meaning "Ternary classification - how this file is categorized"
        teaching "Every file has three states based on its nature"
        format "this <type> classifies as:"

        (1): <positive state - when this condition applies>
        (0): <neutral state - when this condition applies>
        (-1): <negative state - when this condition applies>

    derivation:
        meaning "Lineage - where this file comes from in the template chain"
        teaching "Enables propagation equation tracing: Δsyntax → Δuniversal → Δ[type] → Δ[impl]"
        format "this <type> derives from:"

        original (1): <when establishing new pattern - this IS the source>
        derived (0) from: <parent key> at <parent path>
        variant (-1): <when intentionally departing from parent>

    usage (templates only):
        meaning "Instructions for using this template - only appears in template files"
        format "to use this template:"

        instructions: <how to apply this template>
        meaning: <what files created from this template produce>
        teaching: <key lesson this template demonstrates>

